<div class="wrap">
<header class="mast">
  <p class="eyebrow">Problem brief &middot; greenfield</p>
  <h1>Durable capture of agent coding sessions</h1>
  <p class="deck">What the system must do, and what makes it hard — deliberately without saying how anyone has done it. Hand this to someone who has not seen the existing implementation, and see what they build.</p>
  <div class="prov"><strong>Provenance.</strong> Distilled from research and an implementation spec for a shipping tool, plus production measurements from one large repository. Forces marked <span class="chip">measured</span> were observed in production or reproduced in a harness; the rest are derived. No identifiers, file layouts, vendor names or mechanisms from the existing implementation appear here — that omission is the point, not an oversight.</div>
</header>

<h2><span class="sn">1</span>The problem</h2>
<p>A coding agent works in a version-controlled repository across a series of turns. You want a durable, reviewable record of that work — the conversation, the reasoning, what changed — bound to version-control history, so a person can later point at a commit and ask “what produced this?” and get the session behind it.</p>
<p>The historical assumption was that a human types the commit. Under it, the commit is a <em>curation event</em>: a person decided this work was worth recording, at a moment when the work was finished. Anchoring capture to the commit was therefore correct, and nearly free.</p>
<p>That assumption no longer holds. The agent commits, pushes, and opens pull requests, often several times inside a single turn, with nobody watching. Every decision that quietly depended on <em>“a human chose this moment”</em> is now load-bearing on something untrue.</p>
<p><span class="chip">measured</span> In one production repository over three months, AI-co-authored commits rose from 39% to 62% of non-merge commits quarter over quarter. Of those AI-co-authored commits, <strong>29% carry no link back to any session</strong> — across a dozen authors and four different agents, so systemic rather than one broken setup. Treat the figures as one repository's shape; treat the direction as settled.</p>

<h2><span class="sn">2</span>Domain objects</h2>
<p>Described without committing to a storage model.</p>
<ul>
  <li><span class="term">Session</span> — a continuous working relationship between one person and one agent in one repository. May span hours, many turns, and interruptions.</li>
  <li><span class="term">Turn</span> — one prompt, the agent's work, and the agent stopping. The unit the agent itself understands and signals.</li>
  <li><span class="term">Transcript</span> — the append-only record of the session, <strong>written and owned by the agent, not by you</strong>. You read it while it is being written; you never control its format, location, or lifetime.</li>
  <li><span class="term">Record</span> — the durable artifact you create: a slice of transcript, the files that changed, usage accounting, and enough metadata to render it later.</li>
  <li><span class="term">Link</span> — the bidirectional association between a record and a commit.</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="sn">3</span>Invariants</h2>
<p>Assert these regardless of design.</p>
<ol class="inv">
  <li>Every commit a session produced gets exactly one record, and no record is claimed by two commits.</li>
  <li>The user's working branch is never modified by the capture system.</li>
  <li>Nothing leaves the machine un-redacted, and redaction is configured by the repository — not by the capture process's environment.</li>
  <li>A record exists only for a commit that actually carries its identifier.</li>
  <li><strong>Failure degrades to less data, never to wrong data.</strong> Of everything here, this is the one most often violated by accident.</li>
</ol>

<h2><span class="sn">4</span>The forces</h2>
<p>Each of these broke something real. They are stated as problems, not as answers.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.1</span>The capture trigger and the link anchor are different events</h3>
<p>The commit is the right thing to navigate by: durable, shared, already the unit people reason about. But the <em>turn</em> is when the content is complete. Anchor capture to the commit and you capture a truncated transcript mid-turn and must repair it later. Anchor capture to the turn and the commit happens before you have anything to attach. You will need both, and the seam between them is where most of what follows lives.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.2</span>The identifier must exist before the thing it names</h3>
<p>If the link lives in the commit message, the identifier must be minted before the commit object exists. The identifier and the record therefore cannot be created atomically, and the interval between them is a window in which a reference points at nothing.</p>
<p><span class="chip">measured</span> A crash in that window leaves a permanently dangling reference, undetectable afterwards, because the only evidence the work was ever meant to be recorded was held in memory.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.3</span>Removing the link is a legitimate act, not an error</h3>
<p>If a person can edit the commit message, they can delete the reference — and that must mean <em>do not record this</em>. So any recovery process must materialize a pending record <strong>only</strong> when its identifier appears in a real commit: a positive test. Recovery keyed on “this pending thing is old” resurrects work someone deliberately declined, which is worse than losing it.</p>
<p>The corollary bites. Recovery carries two obligations that pull against each other — finish what was interrupted, discard what was withdrawn — and the discard decision needs <em>positive evidence of absence</em>, not a failed search.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.4</span>Publishing is not a moment you control</h3>
<p>Work can be published mid-turn, before the record is complete, so whatever you publish then is provisional by definition. Two consequences, and getting either wrong is worse than doing nothing: if publishing consumes and discards the pending record, you can never correct what you published; and the publish path can usually be bypassed outright by whoever is doing it, so it is a best-effort barrier, never a guarantee.</p>
<p>Design the correction path first, and treat publishing as an event that may happen at any point — including never again after the first. <span class="chip">measured</span> The common agent shape is commit, push once, open a pull request, never push that branch again — so “it will be corrected on the next push” can mean <em>never</em>.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.5</span>“The repository” is not one directory</h3>
<p>Multiple working directories can share one object store and one session store. Everything that resolves a path must resolve it against the working directory the <strong>event</strong> came from, not whichever one the capture process happens to be running in.</p>
<p><span class="chip">measured</span> Getting this half-right is worse than not fixing it. A partial fix — correcting path filtering but not content resolution — produced records claiming a file was touched while storing the <em>unmodified</em> content from the wrong directory, and silently omitting files that existed only in the right one. Neither failure announced itself.</p>
<p>The lesson generalizes: when several independent places resolve the same fact, converting some leaves the rest silently disagreeing — and a disagreement can invert the meaning of what you store.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.6</span>Configuration must follow the same root as content</h3>
<p>Whatever decides redaction lives in the repository. If your notion of “where the repository is” moves, your configuration moves with it — possibly to a working directory that does not contain the config file at all, where you fall back to defaults.</p>
<div class="warn"><p>This is the most dangerous force here: it is silent, and it sits on the path that decides what leaves the machine. <span class="chip">measured</span> A configuration file deliberately excluded from version control <em>cannot</em> be present in a freshly created working directory — so for repositories configured that way, the fallback is not an edge case. It is every turn, always.</p></div>

<h3><span class="fn">4.7</span>Usage accounting has structurally incompatible sources</h3>
<p>Agents report token or cost usage in at least three shapes: inline in the transcript (positionally addressable), out-of-band as a lump for a whole turn or session (no position at all), and as cumulative-since-start snapshots that must <em>replace</em> rather than accumulate.</p>
<p>Any scheme that apportions usage by position in the transcript works for exactly one of the three. Decide early whether usage is a first-class per-record quantity or a best-effort annotation — retrofitting that distinction is expensive, and the failure mode (usage attributed to the wrong record, or dropped) is invisible in review.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.8</span>The trigger is unreliable, and its failures are silent</h3>
<p>Whatever mechanism causes your code to run when the agent acts can be bypassed by a flag, disabled by the agent's own runtime, or simply absent for tools that reach the same effect another way.</p>
<p><span class="chip">measured</span> One agent release began forcing configuration that disabled the trigger on every command it ran — silently, and invisibly to the tool's own self-diagnosis, because that diagnosis inspected its own environment rather than the agent's.</p>
<p>Plan a reconciliation path that does not depend on the trigger having fired: something that notices, after the fact, that history moved without a record being produced, and says so loudly.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.9</span>Concurrency is the normal case</h3>
<p>Several agents working in several working directories of one repository, writing records simultaneously, is the workflow being encouraged — not a contrived race. Any last-writer-wins update to shared state will lose records, and will lose them silently.</p>

<h3><span class="fn">4.10</span>Nobody is at the terminal</h3>
<p>Prompts, warnings and one-shot notices land in the agent's output, where no person reads them — and a notice that fires once per day is <em>consumed</em> by the agent seeing it. Consent and notification must be repository policy and queryable state, not an interactive question; anything worth telling a person must survive until a person actually looks.</p>

<h2><span class="sn">5</span>Anti-patterns, learned expensively</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Making an unsafe mechanism safe by adding conditions,</strong> rather than choosing a mechanism that needs none. If a design is accumulating gates, the gates are evidence about the mechanism.</li>
  <li><strong>A check that runs once and then stops checking.</strong> It converts a live question into stale confidence.</li>
  <li><strong>A verification that is itself unexecuted.</strong> Worth <em>less</em> than no verification: no verification leaves you looking, a silently-failed one makes you stop.</li>
  <li><strong>Tests asserting that an event occurred rather than what value resulted.</strong> “A record was produced” passes when the record holds the wrong content.</li>
  <li><strong>Recorded-payload fixtures as the only defence against vendor behaviour change.</strong> Fixtures pin a payload; the failures that matter come from behaviour changing around an unchanged payload. Something must exercise the real agent.</li>
  <li><strong>A permanently failing or flaky check.</strong> Zero signal — nobody can distinguish new breakage from old.</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="sn">6</span>What is deliberately not specified</h2>
<div class="ask">
  <p>The point of this brief. We are not describing our storage layout, trigger mechanism, process model, or how a record binds to a commit — and these four are where an independent answer would be worth most.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Must the link live in the commit message at all?</b> Everything in 4.2 and 4.3 follows from a pre-minted identifier written into the commit. A design binding records to commits after the fact dissolves that whole family of problems and acquires different ones. Which set is cheaper?</li>
    <li><b>Should records live inside the repository or beside it?</b> In-repository records travel with a clone and survive the tool being uninstalled; adjacent records never collide with the user's history.</li>
    <li><b>Push or pull?</b> Should agent activity push records into existence as it works, or should records be reconstructed on demand from a transcript that already exists? The second is far less machinery and far more dependent on transcripts you do not own.</li>
    <li><b>What is the smallest thing worth recording?</b> We assumed the commit. A record per turn, per session, or per pull request are all defensible, and imply very different systems.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h2><span class="sn">7</span>How the result would be judged</h2>
<p>Not by resemblance to any existing implementation. By:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Linkage rate</strong> on commits an agent authored, measured on a real repository rather than a fixture.</li>
  <li><strong>Behaviour when the trigger is bypassed</strong> — is the gap detected and reported, or silently absent?</li>
  <li><strong>Crash mid-turn</strong> — is interrupted work recoverable, and is a withdrawn link correctly <em>not</em> recovered?</li>
  <li><strong>A second working directory</strong> — is content captured, and captured <em>correctly</em>, or merely captured?</li>
  <li><strong>Whether wrong data can be produced at all</strong>, under any force in section 4. Less data is acceptable; a record that misdescribes what happened is not.</li>
</ul>

